FLOORFORGE_41 — rendered text contrast: 4 real WCAG 1.4.3 failures, and a
checker that was hiding them under 324 false positives.

THE DEFECT

audit/scripts/contrast.mjs measures the contrast of text as it is actually
painted — it samples the background from a screenshot rather than walking the
DOM, so text over images, gradients and translucent scrims is measured the way a
person sees it. It is the only check that can judge those cases: axe-core
deliberately declines to score text over an image, because it cannot know what
is underneath.

It was reporting 45 failures. It also exits non-zero, which means the site had a
red check nobody was reading — the worst state for a test to be in.

41 of those 45 were not real:

  279 text runs      `sr-only` content. Tailwind's utility lays the glyphs out
  (per full run)     inside a 1x1 clipped box. The glyphs still have a normal
                     Range rect, so they survived the "is this bigger than 2px"
                     filter, got sampled against whatever sat behind the clipped
                     box — usually plain white — and were reported as 1:1
                     failures. Nothing is painted. WCAG 1.4.3 covers text "that
                     is displayed"; this is not displayed.

  88 text runs       Cards clipped out of view by the showcase rail, which is
  (per full run)     `overflow-x: auto` and extends well past the 1440px capture.
                     A card off the right edge still reports a normal rect whose
                     centre can land a few pixels outside the rail, in the white
                     page margin. White label text measured against that margin
                     scored 1.00:1 and was reported as the site's worst contrast
                     defect. Scroll the rail and the same label sits on a dark
                     render at 9:1.

4 were real, and every one is a near-miss — the kind no one finds by eye:

  4.44:1   /                showcase card label, white on bg-black/45 over a
                            photographic render
  4.23:1   /simulator       "Drag to orbit · scroll to zoom · right-drag to pan"
  4.49:1   /pro-simulator   the concept-teardown caption, white/45
  4.43:1   /pro-simulator   same caption where it crosses the darker panel

THE FIXES

Tool (audit/scripts/contrast.mjs):
  * `isVisuallyHidden()` — skips anything clipped away, detected by the clip
    signature rather than by class name, so it also catches hand-rolled
    visually-hidden helpers and survives a Tailwind rename.
  * `visibleRect()` — intersects each run with every clipping ancestor and
    samples the centre of what SURVIVES. Generalises the old
    `x >= screenshotWidth` guard, which only caught runs overflowing the
    DOCUMENT, not ones clipped by a container inside it. A run less than half
    visible, or under 8px wide, is skipped: the sample point would land on the
    container's own edge and measure the frame, not the backdrop.
  * Both counts are REPORTED (`visuallyHiddenSkipped`, `clippedOutOfViewSkipped`)
    rather than silently dropped, so a reader can sanity-check the exclusions
    instead of taking "0 failures" on faith.

Product:
  * ShowcaseCarousel label scrim bg-black/45 -> bg-black/70. 45% cannot guarantee
    anything: over a white render it composites to rgb(140,140,140) and white
    text on that is 1.9:1. At 70% the worst case ANY render can produce is
    rgb(77,77,77) — 8.5:1 — so the label is legible over every frame in the
    library and every frame added later. This is a floor, not a nudge past 4.5.
  * Simulator hint: --muted-foreground -> --foreground at 75%. The token is
    validated against --bg (4.76:1) and --muted (4.55:1); the hint sits on the
    WebGL clear colour #eef2f6, which is darker than both and is not a token at
    all, so the validated pair never applied. 4.23:1 -> 7.4:1.
  * ProTeardown caption: white/45 -> white/60, 4.43:1 -> 6.6:1 on the darker of
    the two panels it crosses. This line carries the "not telemetry from
    shipping hardware" disclaimer — the last text on the site that should be
    hard to read. Per the standing constraint the disclaimer was made MORE
    legible; no word of it changed.

No colour token was redefined, no layout moved, no copy changed, and the amber
industrial identity is untouched.

VERIFIED against a production build served on :3111

  contrast          45 failing -> 0 failing, exit 1 -> exit 0
                    1345 runs measured, 279 sr-only + 88 clipped excluded and
                    reported
  token-contrast    37 pairs, 0 FAIL (no token was changed)
  axe-core          0 violations, 11 routes
  responsive        0 FAIL — 320->1920 continuous, reflow 320x256, 200% text
  structure         clean      tap-targets  clean
  input-font-size   clean      parse-check  clean
  verifyapplied.sh  76 in, 0 out (7 new assertions added for this patch)
  tsc --noEmit      clean
  eslint            0 errors (1 pre-existing warning, lib/db/client.ts:172)
  next build        20/20 routes

REPORTED, NOT FIXED

With the false positives gone the tightest PASSING margins on the site are
4.51:1 against a 4.5:1 requirement — one hundredth of a point. They are
--muted-foreground on white, the site's most common pair, measured in situ on
small badge text. They pass. But a pair with no margin fails the moment a
background shifts by one step, and raising --muted-foreground is a change to the
whole design system and a brand judgement, so it is yours to make rather than
mine to slip into a contrast patch.

